    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter the August 27 slate with a core offense anchored by two elite projections, **Yordan Alvarez** (65.89) and **Lars Nootbaar** (64.87). **Yordan Alvarez** remains a must-start despite facing a formidable arm in Gerrit Cole; his elite xwOBA of .458 and the significant boost from batting in the #2 spot mitigate the SP skill penalty. Meanwhile, **Lars Nootbaar** offers the highest ceiling of the day, surging to a 64.87 score thanks to his leadoff role and a favorable matchup against Landen Roupp, where the dynamic platoon advantage provides an 11% lift. **Kazuma Okamoto** (59.66) also warrants attention as a strong play; he benefits from a rare alignment of park factors and a plus-matchup against Noah Cameron, making him the most reliable option in the lower half of the lineup.\n\nOur bench decisions today were heavily dictated by the absence of several key speed threats. With **Trea Turner**, **Pete Crow-Armstrong**, and **Kyle Schwarber** sidelined due to team off-days, our stolen base efficiency takes a hit, placing a premium on the remaining active roster. **Christian Yelich** (45.09) occupied the most precarious spot in the lineup; while his projection dipped due to a negative platoon split against Sean Manaea, he narrowly edged out marginal options like **Gary Sanchez** (48.65) and **Christian Encarnacion-Strand** (50.70) in terms of overall roster construction necessity. **Carson Benge** (40.05) remains the riskiest start, as his base projection was severely hampered by a matchup against Jacob Misiorowski (2.14 xERA). Owners should monitor the \"Lineup Pending\" status for **Gary Sanchez**, **Christian Encarnacion-Strand**, and **Kazuma Okamoto** closely, as any downward shift in their projected batting order could push them toward the \"Below floor\" threshold, potentially necessitating a pivot to depth pieces like **Caleb Durbin**, **Colt Keith**, or **Josh Smith** if they remain available in other configurations.",
